opMixT -- SEQ 347 R3 (Tony, 2026-10-12): MIXED OPERANDS. A count or NUMBER on the left reads a STRING or TOKEN on the right
as its count (its text length): 3 + "12" is 5, 3.5 + "12" is 5.5. A STRING or TOKEN on the left concatenates the right
side's text: "ab" + "cd" is "abcd", "12" + 3 is "123". L5-L8 are the same with TOKENs. SEQ 348 R1: a TOKEN is minted
from a parse, never by getLine (getLine's TOKEN spans its line's terminator, which insertAt needs): each is the label of a
TOKENize rule (TkTwelve, TkAB, TkCD) handed back by tell() -- captureSpan gives it exactly the matched text, kind 14. tell's verdict carries the
label in `reply`, a GROUP holding it: `rp = *v["reply"]` copies that holder and `*rp` is the label itself, one star per
statement because the old road (switch off) does not compose `**`. Copying `*v["reply"]` alone would make the TOKEN a
holder, which reads as kind 14 at top level and as a group in an operand. The
rows OM T1-T3 print each TOKEN before it is used.
Four cells per line: jitF the jitted first fire into a target with NO kind (it takes the result's kind, R0); jitR a refire
into the now-kinded target (it keeps it); int and int2 the same two on the interpreted road, run last (bear-trap 25).
Each cell prints OM <line> <cell> then OMV <text> <datA>: 5 count, 9 NUMBER, 13 STRING, 14 TOKEN, 0 none.
